Implement the Verilog module based on the following description.
You are given a 24-bit input vector in[23:0]. We want to know some relationships between each bit and its neighbour: 
// (1) out_left: Each bit of this output vector should indicate whether both the corresponding input bit and its neighbour to the left are '1'. For example, out_left[22] should indicate if in[22] and in[23] are both 1. Since in[23] has no neighbour to the left, the answer is obvious so we don't need to know out_left[23]. 
// (2) out_right: Each bit of this output vector should indicate whether any of the corresponding input bit and its neighbour to the right are '1'. For example, out_right[2] should indicate if either in[2] or in[1] are 1. Since in[0] has no neighbour to the right, the answer is obvious so we don't need to know out_right[0]. 
// (3) out_xor: Each bit of this output vector should indicate whether the corresponding input bit is different from its neighbour to the left. For example, out_xor[22] should indicate if in[22] is different from in[23]. For this part, treat the vector as wrapping around, so in[23]'s neighbour to the left is in[0].

module top_module (
	input [23:0] in,
	output [22:0] out_left,
	output [23:1] out_right,
	output [23:0] out_xor);

